<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ashurnasirpal II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 883–859 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ashurnasirpal II was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> from 883 to 859 BCE and one of the founders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-assyrian-empire">Neo-Assyrian Empire</a>. In yearly campaigns he subdued the Aramaean states along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khabur-river">Khabur</a> and the highlands of Nairi and Zamua, and reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, collecting tribute from Patina and the Phoenician cities. His inscriptions boast of brutal punishment of rebels. He rebuilt Kalhu, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimrud">Nimrud</a>, as his capital.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashurnasirpal-ii.jpg" alt="Ashurnasirpal II" width="320" height="324" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ashurnasipal_with_official.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ashurnasirpal II, whose name Aššur-nāṣir-apli means &quot;Ashur is guardian of the heir&quot;, was the third king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-assyrian-empire">Neo-Assyrian Empire</a>. He succeeded his father, Tukulti-Ninurta II, and reigned from 883 to 859 BCE. His queen was Mullissu-mukannišat-Ninua, and his son and successor was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-iii">Shalmaneser III</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Ashurnasirpal began a vast programme of expansion. He conquered peoples to the north as far as Nairi, exacted tribute from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a>, and invaded Aram, subduing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaeans">Aramaeans</a> and Neo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khabur-river">Khabur</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>. Like earlier Assyrian kings he campaigned along the Euphrates against the Aramaeans and in the Diyala against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. His army was typically made up of infantry, including auxiliaries and foreigners, heavy and light cavalry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a>. He installed Assyrian governors rather than relying on local client rulers who paid tribute, and where revolts recurred the local monarch was replaced by a governor loyal only to the Assyrian king.</p><p>His inscriptions record brutal treatment of rebels. After crushing a revolt in a two-day pitched battle, he states that he cut off the hands, feet, ears, noses and lips of prisoners, made heaps of their ears and heads, and burned children. After this victory he advanced without opposition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and exacted tribute from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a>. He did not destroy the Phoenician cities he conquered, and his siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> failed. Tribute from them supplied iron for weapons, Lebanese cedar for building, and gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> to pay troops. Of resettled populations he wrote that he imposed more tribute and tax than ever before, in horses, mules, oxen, sheep, wine and labour.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Ashurnasirpal moved his capital to Kalhu, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimrud">Nimrud</a>, using enslaved captives to build it. His palace there probably began about 878 BCE and was completed after around fifteen years, with the near-total rebuilding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-assyrian-empire">Middle Assyrian</a> city. An inscription describing its inauguration records a ten-day banquet attended by 69,574 people. Its walls were lined with carved alabaster reliefs of the king with winged protective spirits, hunting lions and campaigning. Each bore the Standard Inscription, which traces his lineage, recounts his victories, defines his empire and tells how he founded Kalhu.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austen-henry-layard">Austen Henry Layard</a> rediscovered the palace in 1845 and sent many reliefs to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, most to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/max-mallowan">Max Mallowan</a> re-excavated the site from 1947 to 1957. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze</a> bands from the gates at Balawat, 6.8 metres high, show his reign; one set was found by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuzd-rassam">Hormuzd Rassam</a> in 1878.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ashurnasirpal II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the ninth century BCE it paid tribute to the Assyrian Empire: <b>Ashurnasirpal II</b> received its gifts on his march to the Mediterranean, and later kings of Byblos appear in the tribute lists of Tiglath-Pileser III and Sennacherib.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ashurnasirpal II</b> of Assyria took tribute from Tyre, Sidon, Byblos and Arwad about 870 BCE, and the bronze gates of his son Shalmaneser III at Balawat show Tyrian tribute ferried from the island.</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>King Ahuni led resistance to <b>Ashurnasirpal II</b> and Shalmaneser III of the Assyrian Empire, forming alliances with Carchemish and other Syrian kingdoms.</blockquote><a href="/state/bit-adini">Bit Adini</a></li><li><blockquote>Ammi-ba'al made a treaty with <b>Ashurnasirpal II</b> of the Assyrian Empire but was murdered by rebels in 879 BCE, prompting an Assyrian campaign.</blockquote><a href="/state/bit-zamani">Bit Zamani</a></li><li><blockquote>It was a recognised province of Babylonia by the 9th century BCE, and <b>Ashurnasirpal II</b> of the Assyrian Empire campaigned against it in 878 BCE when it sheltered his enemies.</blockquote><a href="/state/land-of-suhu">Land of Suhu</a></li><li><blockquote>Repeated Assyrian campaigns under <b>Ashurnasirpal II</b> and Shalmaneser III pushed the Nairi chiefdoms to unite.</blockquote><a href="/state/nairi">Nairi</a></li></ul></section>
